> “The platform uses one evidence-based learning engine, but each learner’s pathway is governed by the curriculum, language, classroom practice, connectivity, and family expectations of the local market. Teachers and parents can understand and influence the system, while local educators determine what the platform teaches and how it is expressed.”

The company should therefore centralize the technology, data discipline, security, and learning science while localizing curriculum, language, pedagogy, trust, support, pricing, and procurement. In 2026, success should mean not launching everywhere at once, but proving a repeatable model in Vietnam and France, validating Saudi Arabia through controlled private-sector deployment, and entering Kenya through partners capable of funding and supporting the operational realities of the market.

