# 8. Communications and creator strategy

## 8.1 Core positioning

> **Asian formulation expertise, adapted to the way local consumers wear, share, and experience beauty.**

Proof should come from:

- local wear testing;
- local shade and undertone validation;
- Portuguese or Spanish education;
- credible local creators;
- transparent ingredients;
- verified customer reviews;
- reliable fulfillment;
- honest product limitations.

The brand should show local consumers as the protagonists. Asian heritage explains the product origin and formulation philosophy; it should not be used to imply cultural or aesthetic superiority.

## 8.2 Campaign waves

### Wave 1: credibility before reach

Objectives:

- establish authenticity;
- explain the product;
- test language and claims;
- gather shade and usage feedback.

Tactics:

- creator seeding;
- professional testing;
- local advisory panels;
- waitlists;
- sample programs;
- product education;
- closed social-commerce sessions.

### Wave 2: demonstration and conversion

Objectives:

- make performance visible;
- reduce trial risk;
- produce attributable sales.

Tactics:

- wear tests;
- texture and application videos;
- shade matching;
- routine builders;
- live shopping;
- affiliate links;
- marketplace reviews;
- WhatsApp consultations.

### Wave 3: retention and advocacy

Objectives:

- drive second purchase;
- establish routines;
- turn customers into credible advocates.

Tactics:

- post-purchase tutorials;
- replenishment reminders;
- review requests;
- loyalty benefits;
- referral offers;
- customer routine content;
- adviser follow-up.

## 8.3 Creator portfolio and test budget

Use a diversified portfolio rather than one celebrity-dependent plan:

- 10% of creator budget for professional authorities;
- 45% for micro- and mid-tier demonstration creators;
- 25% for community and city-specific creators;
- 20% for larger reach creators after conversion proof.

For planning, reserve an initial creator test budget equivalent to approximately **5–10% of the first six months’ expected net revenue**, with a separate production and paid-amplification budget. Start with small paid tests and expand based on net contribution, not views.

Compensation should combine:

- fixed content fees where appropriate;
- product gifting for genuinely voluntary seeding;
- affiliate commissions on net paid sales;
- usage-rights fees;
- paid amplification or whitelisting fees;
- bonuses for repeat purchase and compliant performance.

Every agreement should specify:

- disclosure requirements;
- claims and substantiation rules;
- content approval level;
- usage rights and duration;
- paid-media amplification;
- exclusivity category and duration;
- competitor restrictions;
- attribution window;
- treatment of returns and cancellations;
- takedown and crisis obligations.

Do not call a paid or contractually obligated post “organic.” Gifting should not be represented as independent editorial endorsement.

## 8.4 Country executions

### Mexico

Lead with:

- simple routine education;
- skincare and makeup preparation;
- practical wear tests;
- authenticity and delivery reassurance;
- creator-to-marketplace and creator-to-owned-store journeys.

Use TikTok, Instagram, YouTube Shorts, Mercado Libre content, and WhatsApp support according to current platform availability and policy.

### Colombia

Lead with:

- makeup-artist demonstrations;
- wear and finish testing;
- multiple coverage and application options;
- creator affiliates;
- live shopping;
- WhatsApp consultations;
- community and event-ready bundles.

### Brazil

Lead with:

- native Portuguese education;
- texture, finish, and climate testing;
- shade and undertone proof;
- Pix and installment convenience;
- official marketplace stores;
- Brazilian creators and professional artists;
- live-commerce pilots only after customer service and fulfillment can support demand.

