# 12. Operating timeline

## Q1: validation and readiness

Across all three markets:

- complete consumer segmentation and creator research;
- test positioning and Asian-origin messaging;
- select 8–12 opening SKUs;
- classify every product and claim;
- appoint importers or responsible local entities;
- obtain regulatory and labeling advice;
- quote taxes, duties, 3PL, returns, and marketplace fees;
- complete shade and climate testing;
- build country-specific data and consent flows;
- recruit small creator advisory cohorts.

Brazil should not proceed to inventory commitment until its SKU-level pathway is confirmed.

## Q2: controlled pre-launch

### Mexico

- finalize Spanish packaging and product pages;
- test owned checkout, Mercado Libre, Amazon Mexico, and payment alternatives;
- seed creators and professionals;
- run samples and waitlists;
- test delivery and shade-exchange processes.

### Colombia

- recruit creator and makeup-artist cohort;
- test affiliate attribution and WhatsApp-assisted selling;
- validate PSE, wallets, cards, delivery coverage, and returns;
- run small community and live-commerce pilots.

### Brazil

- complete Portuguese labeling and regulatory steps;
- establish local tax, invoicing, importer, and 3PL arrangements;
- test Pix, installments, domestic delivery, customer service, and returns;
- run content and sample tests only where legally permissible.

## Q3: market-specific go-live

### Mexico

Full launch through:

- owned D2C;
- official Mercado Libre store;
- Amazon Mexico;
- creator and adviser content;
- limited physical sampling.

### Colombia

Focused launch through:

- owned D2C;
- Mercado Libre;
- creator affiliates;
- WhatsApp consultation;
- makeup-artist and community demonstrations.

### Brazil

Launch only if every priority SKU has:

- the required regulatory status;
- compliant Portuguese packaging;
- local responsible-party coverage;
- tax and invoicing readiness;
- domestic fulfillment;
- tested customer support and returns.

If not, continue a compliant waitlist, research, and content-building program rather than forcing a commercial launch.

## Q4: selective scale

- increase inventory only for products meeting contribution and repeat thresholds;
- expand shade ranges using demand and mismatch data;
- add marketplace platforms only where incremental economics are positive;
- introduce loyalty and replenishment;
- assess salon, professional, and adviser partnerships;
- reduce creator, marketplace, or paid-media spend that cannot meet margin requirements;
- publish a local customer-review and product-performance library.

